[{"id":"VRCE-2-30-1","kanda":2,"kandaId":"ayodhya","sarga":30,"verse":1,"english":"Having distributed much wealth to the Brāhmaṇas, the Rāghavas set out with $ītā for the purpose of seeing their father.","alignment":{"type":"1:1","confidence":0.873,"method":"shared-sanskrit-ngram","duttRefs":["DUTT-2-33-1"],"status":"unreviewed"},"source":{"sanskritLocator":"2030001","sanskritFile":"Ram02.txt","englishEdition":"M. N. Dutt 1891–94"},"sanskrit_fingerprints":{"exact":"fa93639f848ae47f841eb1cb640f6306319c1b577b0a2d9605279ff0bc55c91b","compact":"5f039bff39aa9fc3c076bf6d60b3eaef38d77c029ca5bd52699f9b2daccebb67","nasal":"5f039bff39aa9fc3c076bf6d60b3eaef38d77c029ca5bd52699f9b2daccebb67"}},{"id":"VRCE-2-30-2","kanda":2,"kandaId":"ayodhya","sarga":30,"verse":2,"english":"The two looked beautiful with a couple of handmaids (following them), taking the weapons that had been decked by Sītā with flowery wreaths.","alignment":{"type":"1:1","confidence":0.633,"method":"shared-sanskrit-ngram","duttRefs":["DUTT-2-33-2"],"status":"unreviewed"},"source":{"sanskritLocator":"2030002","sanskritFile":"Ram02.txt","englishEdition":"M. N. Dutt 1891–94"},"sanskrit_fingerprints":{"exact":"ed884e3d7633971b274c5e76c281b0eb647b8cb857f6af736dd01d6c9562a3c8","compact":"5a0a25a19f2ddd0b88250940fcc58be693dc531d3f0004e9347944d4a94b55e2","nasal":"5a0a25a19f2ddd0b88250940fcc58be693dc531d3f0004e9347944d4a94b55e2"}},{"id":"VRCE-2-30-3","kanda":2,"kandaId":"ayodhya","sarga":30,"verse":3,"english":"Then crowds of elegantly-attired citizens, mounting on the terraces of three-storied and seven-storied houses, looked on the scene with hearts filled with sorrow.","alignment":{"type":"1:1","confidence":0.661,"method":"shared-sanskrit-ngram","duttRefs":["DUTT-2-33-3"],"status":"unreviewed"},"source":{"sanskritLocator":"2030003","sanskritFile":"Ram02.txt","englishEdition":"M. N. Dutt 1891–94"},"sanskrit_fingerprints":{"exact":"8b1d1bdd374f6f0f6824a85b948b4f7cab0262f57d597579a387360251547f7c","compact":"493ff8c9668956bf6a1bfd1981141cb587cf6ed95028f34bf66af784c63eb1d2","nasal":"493ff8c9668956bf6a1bfd1981141cb587cf6ed95028f34bf66af784c63eb1d2"}},{"id":"VRCE-2-30-4","kanda":2,"kandaId":"ayodhya","sarga":30,"verse":4,"english":"Unable to thread the streets because of a vast concourse, they ascending the terraces of the buildings, eyed Rāghava with woebegone eyes.","alignment":{"type":"1:1","confidence":0.764,"method":"shared-sanskrit-ngram","duttRefs":["DUTT-2-33-4"],"status":"unreviewed"},"source":{"sanskritLocator":"2030004","sanskritFile":"Ram02.txt","englishEdition":"M. N. Dutt 1891–94"},"sanskrit_fingerprints":{"exact":"9a9ba50d82b95d0935366074be7e15a91a0cf4b2314bd6a895d3a149fce18f4c","compact":"fa4ac4ac22c78c32721af9e31116aea3625f0f6a4e244cecc2d9d5e306c83c04","nasal":"d5fff693a1bfb14f3e669c615b42223062ff4de8d41898341f64274cec537c1b"}},{"id":"VRCE-2-30-5","kanda":2,"kandaId":"ayodhya","sarga":30,"verse":5,"english":"And deprived of their senses by grief, the multitudes,* beholding Rāma proceeding on foot in company with Sītā and his younger brother, said. *Another reading i.e., Beholding Rāma without his umbrella and walking a foot.","alignment":{"type":"approximate","confidence":0.22,"method":"shared-sanskrit-ngram","duttRefs":["DUTT-2-33-5"],"status":"unreviewed"},"source":{"sanskritLocator":"2030005","sanskritFile":"Ram02.txt","englishEdition":"M. N. Dutt 1891–94"},"sanskrit_fingerprints":{"exact":"8a474f3f3444aced7556ca84d7888b9629b37b4bb8d83ef5cebb5b1d51227b4c","compact":"33594bc5841ec14d758e6a3a234312f27883609f26554c3f4064bb6fc870433a","nasal":"33594bc5841ec14d758e6a3a234312f27883609f26554c3f4064bb6fc870433a"}},{"id":"VRCE-2-30-6","kanda":2,"kandaId":"ayodhya","sarga":30,"verse":6,"english":"He that used to be followed by the vast body of the four-fold forces, proceeds now along with Sita, followed by Laksmana alone.","alignment":{"type":"1:1","confidence":1.0,"method":"shared-sanskrit-ngram","duttRefs":["DUTT-2-33-6"],"status":"unreviewed"},"source":{"sanskritLocator":"2030006","sanskritFile":"Ram02.txt","englishEdition":"M. N. Dutt 1891–94"},"sanskrit_fingerprints":{"exact":"639b83c3d5508dc3e7f13ca8dfe6f680a6b60e2cbe4ada3a0a6cf93b6793dcbf","compact":"523d5116fd0b55f47973eb9d7686245217970569df26f16960dff80e848eadd9","nasal":"6139abd77dc7b461eba1714167d1a0b6a7fe3c10e27b22e39c98c882bbfed25f"}},{"id":"VRCE-2-30-7","kanda":2,"kandaId":"ayodhya","sarga":30,"verse":7,"english":"Knowing every kind of enjoyment, that magnanimous one who has tasted of every luxury, for maintaining the dignity of morality, does not wish to falsify (his father's) word.","alignment":{"type":"1:1","confidence":0.531,"method":"shared-sanskrit-ngram","duttRefs":["DUTT-2-33-7"],"status":"unreviewed"},"source":{"sanskritLocator":"2030007","sanskritFile":"Ram02.txt","englishEdition":"M. N. Dutt 1891–94"},"sanskrit_fingerprints":{"exact":"5d8f29f5679d210801732a5eb8b0fffd98a877f5c23efbdfcda68b69a96640c3","compact":"24f2b4da152980e1e719c3f7e4b6463a0543f2ee8788be37fac94d6bffa97fe8","nasal":"24f2b4da152980e1e719c3f7e4b6463a0543f2ee8788be37fac94d6bffa97fe8"}},{"id":"VRCE-2-30-8","kanda":2,"kandaId":"ayodhya","sarga":30,"verse":8,"english":"And that Sītā whom formerly the very rangers of the sky could not see, is to-day beheld by the passers-by.","alignment":{"type":"1:1","confidence":1.0,"method":"shared-sanskrit-ngram","duttRefs":["DUTT-2-33-8"],"status":"unreviewed"},"source":{"sanskritLocator":"2030008","sanskritFile":"Ram02.txt","englishEdition":"M. N. Dutt 1891–94"},"sanskrit_fingerprints":{"exact":"1bb9501b8ca20d3e299070a039f22f8159f52754bc2f8f6d3519746fe97ab87f","compact":"7b6503781e21445433f4db328160d20bbbf1ea734c6a4ffaedc60dc252e8a7a8","nasal":"f91d953c685d18347ce781bf76813e71165640bb0a85997641a8e662091c4d6a"}},{"id":"VRCE-2-30-9","kanda":2,"kandaId":"ayodhya","sarga":30,"verse":9,"english":"Now summer's heat and winter's cold and the rains of the wet season wil speedily stain Sītā, whose person is worthy of being dyed, and who used to daub her limbs with red sandal paste.","alignment":{"type":"1:1","confidence":1.0,"method":"shared-sanskrit-ngram","duttRefs":["DUTT-2-33-9"],"status":"unreviewed"},"source":{"sanskritLocator":"2030009","sanskritFile":"Ram02.txt","englishEdition":"M. N. Dutt 1891–94"},"sanskrit_fingerprints":{"exact":"a7288382150b7af0a22377e125bcc5280e768c7d269ed95182a980d9f4a76ae4","compact":"c1cd1ac4fcdde2b0032d39f3c7ea83b83dbd96abe88c3e97da2841371f529186","nasal":"182e20d24dfdaadcd29d4433d10a7b1bb883d24d53f775f7d633d4d2b7e168e0"}},{"id":"VRCE-2-30-10","kanda":2,"kandaId":"ayodhya","sarga":30,"verse":10,"english":"Surely to-day Dasaratha speaks thus, possessed by some evil spirit; for the king ought by no means to banish his beloved son.","alignment":{"type":"1:1","confidence":1.0,"method":"shared-sanskrit-ngram","duttRefs":["DUTT-2-33-10"],"status":"unreviewed"},"source":{"sanskritLocator":"2030010","sanskritFile":"Ram02.txt","englishEdition":"M. N. Dutt 1891–94"},"sanskrit_fingerprints":{"exact":"e4318302a5a494048956347fd903fae002d5c95c34a2f9e5bdeaffd66791fe87","compact":"52b4e25be068dc1bfb1aa955774b5fafaaed8cfa60cf635fe34687ecee2ea2df","nasal":"52b4e25be068dc1bfb1aa955774b5fafaaed8cfa60cf635fe34687ecee2ea2df"}},{"id":"VRCE-2-30-11","kanda":2,"kandaId":"ayodhya","sarga":30,"verse":11,"english":"Who ever exiles his son, albeit he be worthless? And what is to be said concerning a son that has fast secured all men's hearts by his behaviour?","alignment":{"type":"1:1","confidence":0.718,"method":"shared-sanskrit-ngram","duttRefs":["DUTT-2-33-11"],"status":"unreviewed"},"source":{"sanskritLocator":"2030011","sanskritFile":"Ram02.txt","englishEdition":"M. N. Dutt 1891–94"},"sanskrit_fingerprints":{"exact":"139de5341aab81a2bdc224fa10e1520572d511ced2169dda811d196d0f1a89b5","compact":"f371ac764822caadf087a8be27d2be7f567b7425c22d8d378c66c5ff5f7b6f17","nasal":"f371ac764822caadf087a8be27d2be7f567b7425c22d8d378c66c5ff5f7b6f17"}},{"id":"VRCE-2-30-12","kanda":2,"kandaId":"ayodhya","sarga":30,"verse":12,"english":"Universal benevolence, kindness, learning, goodness, the restraint of the senses, and the control of the faculties, these six qualities adorn that best of men, Rāghava.","alignment":{"type":"1:1","confidence":0.889,"method":"shared-sanskrit-ngram","duttRefs":["DUTT-2-33-12"],"status":"unreviewed"},"source":{"sanskritLocator":"2030012","sanskritFile":"Ram02.txt","englishEdition":"M. N. Dutt 1891–94"},"sanskrit_fingerprints":{"exact":"c130ad69ea6d1502d5ea973a048db0e0a8cc1c54b74c5e85d437edcf260a2a64","compact":"74cf69ac20224646bcb9cda7143e90b7725e884d346a088a5aa93153ffa873fd","nasal":"9feb4dbe769a7873f8f0e4cab6f7d69dbeecfac33e6a48238d584bf12cb8d9c8"}},{"id":"VRCE-2-30-13","kanda":2,"kandaId":"ayodhya","sarga":30,"verse":13,"english":"Therefore the subjects will be afflicted in consequence of his separation, even as aquatic animals are, when summer dries up the waters of a tank.","alignment":{"type":"1:1","confidence":1.0,"method":"shared-sanskrit-ngram","duttRefs":["DUTT-2-33-13"],"status":"unreviewed"},"source":{"sanskritLocator":"2030013","sanskritFile":"Ram02.txt","englishEdition":"M. N. Dutt 1891–94"},"sanskrit_fingerprints":{"exact":"7155ddaf2b04762e21139507510c35d0372f8af6084f0b850a825e3af1344023","compact":"0378b91ea0cef8a4b4667e9097e93098ab1877b8a353e13446fa31255371f684","nasal":"0378b91ea0cef8a4b4667e9097e93098ab1877b8a353e13446fa31255371f684"}},{"id":"VRCE-2-30-14","kanda":2,"kandaId":"ayodhya","sarga":30,"verse":14,"english":"The entire earth is distressed on account of the distress of this lord of the earth, even like a tree bearing blossoms and fruits, when its roots have been severed.","alignment":{"type":"1:1","confidence":1.0,"method":"shared-sanskrit-ngram","duttRefs":["DUTT-2-33-14"],"status":"unreviewed"},"source":{"sanskritLocator":"2030014","sanskritFile":"Ram02.txt","englishEdition":"M. N. Dutt 1891–94"},"sanskrit_fingerprints":{"exact":"51862d4ff3b939eaacfd94e72f191ce611ad2fd786196d44ada6007b1f9d68bd","compact":"131c9122314189b9d12bc042428ee8d6c6b395a3e5dcbc5c08125cc7e5ba0219","nasal":"131c9122314189b9d12bc042428ee8d6c6b395a3e5dcbc5c08125cc7e5ba0219"}},{"id":"VRCE-2-30-15","kanda":2,"kandaId":"ayodhya","sarga":30,"verse":15,"english":"Therefore, accompanied by our wives and friends will we like Lakşmaņa follow the departing Rāghava by the same way that he takes.","alignment":{"type":"1:1","confidence":0.836,"method":"shared-sanskrit-ngram","duttRefs":["DUTT-2-33-16"],"status":"unreviewed"},"source":{"sanskritLocator":"2030015","sanskritFile":"Ram02.txt","englishEdition":"M. N. Dutt 1891–94"},"sanskrit_fingerprints":{"exact":"64e78a5b052147d253ad40916f64d4ce795ead6eac0422eed4a99795833ae4d5","compact":"2ae92ee239154dd5333c3ae81021e421ec1c9c2e48cc3623e7b64f5a77131b6e","nasal":"738a4d877842fc42297fe7b128926ae2a6b441d55f0de3c3534cf8137005455e"}},{"id":"VRCE-2-30-16","kanda":2,"kandaId":"ayodhya","sarga":30,"verse":16,"english":"And leaving aside our gardens and fields and abodes, will we, making the righteous Rāma's happiness and misery our own, follow him.","alignment":{"type":"1:1","confidence":1.0,"method":"shared-sanskrit-ngram","duttRefs":["DUTT-2-33-17"],"status":"unreviewed"},"source":{"sanskritLocator":"2030016","sanskritFile":"Ram02.txt","englishEdition":"M. N. Dutt 1891–94"},"sanskrit_fingerprints":{"exact":"8b2b553badc1112c92558f6486794f5e19fa442dae51bd29c5879334d24e0ac0","compact":"1ea4c5067c43c447480bf95bba654cbb2101277926586d6539bd158edacbbf96","nasal":"1ea4c5067c43c447480bf95bba654cbb2101277926586d6539bd158edacbbf96"}},{"id":"VRCE-2-30-17","kanda":2,"kandaId":"ayodhya","sarga":30,"verse":17,"english":"Let Kaikeyi possess herself of our deserted mansions, deprived of their buried treasures, with their un-swept courtyards robbed of kine and wealth, and shorn of all substance, filled with dust, and abandoned by the deities, mansions where rats will run from hole to hole, which will neither emit smoke nor contain water, which will not be swept by broomsticks, from which sacrifices, and the slaughter of sacrificial beasts, and the offering of oblations, and the recitation of sacred texts, and Japa, will be absent, and around which will be strewn broken carthenware, as they are on occasions of political commotion's or the occurrence of natural calamities.","alignment":{"type":"grouped","confidence":1.0,"method":"shared-sanskrit-ngram","duttRefs":["DUTT-2-33-18"],"status":"unreviewed"},"source":{"sanskritLocator":"2030017","sanskritFile":"Ram02.txt","englishEdition":"M. N. Dutt 1891–94"},"sanskrit_fingerprints":{"exact":"39d16b881be4185385f730e2e380938e5fbef0a23efc3bc52f85b71af3f7dd8d","compact":"34c90f1ae7eca148709fad69000acce8019fee5dc723e3671e224191a4ebff5a","nasal":"34c90f1ae7eca148709fad69000acce8019fee5dc723e3671e224191a4ebff5a"}},{"id":"VRCE-2-30-18","kanda":2,"kandaId":"ayodhya","sarga":30,"verse":18,"english":"Let Kaikeyi possess herself of our deserted mansions, deprived of their buried treasures, with their un-swept courtyards robbed of kine and wealth, and shorn of all substance, filled with dust, and abandoned by the deities, mansions where rats will run from hole to hole, which will neither emit smoke nor contain water, which will not be swept by broomsticks, from which sacrifices, and the slaughter of sacrificial beasts, and the offering of oblations, and the recitation of sacred texts, and Japa, will be absent, and around which will be strewn broken carthenware, as they are on occasions of political commotion's or the occurrence of natural calamities.","alignment":{"type":"grouped","confidence":0.551,"method":"shared-sanskrit-ngram","duttRefs":["DUTT-2-33-18"],"status":"unreviewed"},"source":{"sanskritLocator":"2030018","sanskritFile":"Ram02.txt","englishEdition":"M. N. Dutt 1891–94"},"sanskrit_fingerprints":{"exact":"5e9feb96aa194e939f202bb88bd73df9f5d7053e60d636a6fae71068eb100776","compact":"31e1e6cc9ffda2c95eec86c3bdac2c77e2c8be0100d8f97e0556dd69b0f15720","nasal":"31e1e6cc9ffda2c95eec86c3bdac2c77e2c8be0100d8f97e0556dd69b0f15720"}},{"id":"VRCE-2-30-19","kanda":2,"kandaId":"ayodhya","sarga":30,"verse":19,"english":"Let the forest to which Rāghava repairs resemble a city, and let this city renounced by as be converted into a wilderness.","alignment":{"type":"1:1","confidence":0.862,"method":"shared-sanskrit-ngram","duttRefs":["DUTT-2-33-19"],"status":"unreviewed"},"source":{"sanskritLocator":"2030019","sanskritFile":"Ram02.txt","englishEdition":"M. N. Dutt 1891–94"},"sanskrit_fingerprints":{"exact":"81988d7b410b6fd515a574cde99c236efb8e0382d3d55b39572fd8d66d335632","compact":"f94473adc739c89069bf7576433de2b195a0485492e5b90121dad6049f52edde","nasal":"f94473adc739c89069bf7576433de2b195a0485492e5b90121dad6049f52edde"}},{"id":"VRCE-2-30-20","kanda":2,"kandaId":"ayodhya","sarga":30,"verse":20,"english":"Inspired by the fear of us, serpents will leave their holes, and beats and birds the caves of mountains, and elephants and lions the forest. Let them occupy the tracts left behind by us, and let them renounce such abounding in serpents, beasts, and birds, as yield grass, meat, and fruits. Let Kaikeyi (reign in this realm) along with her sons sons and adherents; we, renouncing homes, will dwell in the forest with Raghava.","alignment":{"type":"split","confidence":0.411,"method":"shared-sanskrit-ngram","duttRefs":["DUTT-2-33-20","DUTT-2-33-21"],"status":"unreviewed"},"source":{"sanskritLocator":"2030020","sanskritFile":"Ram02.txt","englishEdition":"M. N. Dutt 1891–94"},"sanskrit_fingerprints":{"exact":"870a3dfea54536ecc1b4849f91220e8113dd3760c45c4a75d77924bba37bb425","compact":"1c439ccfae6164122ad4a42a9bd406ad58de978df79521fd11558b782e4b049f","nasal":"594f2a83ebe859171f8c5857c533169bb48ec6018cda8fbc694d9909bbf5af4f"}},{"id":"VRCE-2-30-21","kanda":2,"kandaId":"ayodhya","sarga":30,"verse":21,"english":"Rāghava heard various words uttered thus by the populace; and having heard them he did not suffer his mind to be agitated. And that righteous one of the prowess of a mad elephant, form a distance began to make for the residence of his father resembiing in brightness a summit of the Kailasa mountain.","alignment":{"type":"1:1","confidence":0.738,"method":"shared-sanskrit-ngram","duttRefs":["DUTT-2-33-22"],"status":"unreviewed"},"source":{"sanskritLocator":"2030021","sanskritFile":"Ram02.txt","englishEdition":"M. N. Dutt 1891–94"},"sanskrit_fingerprints":{"exact":"ec68358421c62d9ab53271eb763276240b1204fb8ebd06aafe8754b8e4c419be","compact":"12fc17ede67514aabcc43119875d3c032b9bf8250364867e1e36428b91693b49","nasal":"12fc17ede67514aabcc43119875d3c032b9bf8250364867e1e36428b91693b49"}},{"id":"VRCE-2-30-22","kanda":2,"kandaId":"ayodhya","sarga":30,"verse":22,"english":"Seeing that well-wisher of his thus depressed, Rāma endeavouring by all means to do his father's bidding, cheerfully went on, desirous of beholding his sire.","alignment":{"type":"1:1","confidence":0.614,"method":"shared-sanskrit-ngram","duttRefs":["DUTT-2-33-24"],"status":"unreviewed"},"source":{"sanskritLocator":"2030022","sanskritFile":"Ram02.txt","englishEdition":"M. N. Dutt 1891–94"},"sanskrit_fingerprints":{"exact":"89271e32cb9816d19933c4c99737a925fd8b75e6ae40aef53d879a970747fbb7","compact":"d9031652fbeda6be91c3ac97f75a87015f802e5bf9df13a0416ba1b65c1d9968","nasal":"374c2ac1845c44aa8f1ab656e83fba6384708ac9253b6d5f365ab59230852f1d"}},{"id":"VRCE-2-30-23","kanda":2,"kandaId":"ayodhya","sarga":30,"verse":23,"english":"And with the view of meeting the aggrieved king before repairing to the forest, the magnanimous son of the Ikşvāku race, seeing Sumantra, stayed there, so that that nobleminded one might inform his father of his visit.","alignment":{"type":"1:1","confidence":0.913,"method":"shared-sanskrit-ngram","duttRefs":["DUTT-2-33-25"],"status":"unreviewed"},"source":{"sanskritLocator":"2030023","sanskritFile":"Ram02.txt","englishEdition":"M. N. Dutt 1891–94"},"sanskrit_fingerprints":{"exact":"6cb5b5cc62b1de8f868ddf58989f4e404632c6f20a5431ab9d7496161776267b","compact":"a656eb025badb7fdb92a7a102bf7aab2044a7c5c8c1bd78362f903d4d0589f0d","nasal":"29525bccdd8f2e440c0198a64c5d98758de3613ff9817d28a57ad853c88fe5bd"}},{"id":"VRCE-2-30-24","kanda":2,"kandaId":"ayodhya","sarga":30,"verse":24,"english":"And making up his mind to go to the woods in accordance with the command of his father, Rāghava seeing Sumantra, said to him; “Do you inform the king of my arrival. to","alignment":{"type":"1:1","confidence":0.6,"method":"shared-sanskrit-ngram","duttRefs":["DUTT-2-33-26"],"status":"unreviewed"},"source":{"sanskritLocator":"2030024","sanskritFile":"Ram02.txt","englishEdition":"M. N. Dutt 1891–94"},"sanskrit_fingerprints":{"exact":"a65821b32439b6ef4899315744ecb3bfc73c6a464f3df360613a526fa0a7deb4","compact":"598e74426c3e35d6bb2e84fbad9752610f0cf718b236a554cd610048e5674ccb","nasal":"a4149592a4a068c7b504f3cd8720b2a0c6eb4798b91c1a1835a9621f60e2524f"}}]
